Travel Advisory Level 2: Exercise Increased Caution Travel Advisory Level 2: Exercise increased cautionin the United Kingdom due to terrorism. Read More...
Ambassador Jeffrey Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam Chair, not only for your leadership, but for your patience in what is I think you’re hearing in polite diplomatic language, a very frustrating five-week first resumed session. Perhaps I will get into that in a couple of minutes.
But colleagues, the United States approached this session with clear expectations: that we would deliver decisions that strengthen accountability, impose discipline, and ensure that resources entrusted to the United Nations are used effectively. While some progress has been made, the overall outcome, as we’ve heard today and you’re going to hear from other delegations, falls woefully short of these standards.
On human resources management, we regret that consensus remained out of reach. Meaningful reform in this area is essential to building a workforce that is agile, accountable, and performance-driven. This includes addressing the overreliance on continuing contracts and ensuring that contractual frameworks support flexibility, merit, and effective management, and on prioritizing workforce rejuvenation to ensure the organization can attract, develop, and retain the next generation of talent.
We are disappointed – and that’s polite diplomatic speak – We are disappointed that once again this Committee was unable to advance common-sense reforms on business class travel. I’ve only been doing this for six months, but boy the UN loves its business class travel. The current standard exceeds that of most national governments and is not a responsible use of taxpayer resources. The continued inability to align travel policies with common sense standards sends absolutely the wrong signal – not only to here, but I think more importantly to the people that we meet in the field doing the hard work on the ground in places like DRC, Central African Republic, Kenya, and other places that I’ve had the privilege to visit in this job. And we therefore call on the Secretary-General in his role of Chief Administrative Officer to ban the use of business class travel.
We underscore the importance of the Fifth Committee meeting its fundamental responsibility to provide sufficient and timely funding for mandated special political missions. These missions carry out critical work, often in fragile and rapidly evolving environments. We applaud this Committee for taking action to ensure funding for the Iran Sanctions Committee and its Panel of Experts.
Finally, we are also concerned by the fact that the 5C has used only half of its allotted slots this session, which again calls into question the need for five weeks. Pretty common sense. Extending the session has not, in it of itself, produced better outcomes. On the contrary, it has diluted focus and discipline. We should return to a four-week session and ensure that our work is structured to deliver results within that timeframe. Or – this is what I would do – given our poor record of achievement, perhaps we should consider doing away with the first resumed entirely. Surely, the money we spend here could be put to better use. If the number I heard was correct, something like $750,000 dollars to have five weeks of sessions to accomplish very little.
Taken together, these outcomes reflect a broader challenge: our collective ability to translate words into actions; commitments to reform into concrete decisions. UN80 has provided a clear direction of travel. It is now incumbent – indeed it’s essential for this Committee to match that ambition with action.
The United States remains committed to working with all delegations to advance a United Nations that is leaner, more accountable, and more effective in delivering results on the ground.
Thank you, Madam Chair.
