Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
CB COTTON: Today a bipartisan group of lawmakers heard more about the work that the White House is putting behind the slogan, MUNGA, make the United Nations great again. The U.S. Ambassador to the UN Mike Waltz tells Fox he’s ready to clean house.
AMBASSADOR MIKE WALTZ: A UN that is leaner, that is less bureaucratic, that is focused on its original mission of keeping the peace… The UN budget has quadrupled in the last 25 years, and we haven’t seen a quadrupling of peace.
COTTON: Ambassador Waltz spoke exclusively to Fox News ahead of the hearing where he described a bloated UN with wasteful spending.
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: We need to reduce the UN’s size and assure every taxpayer dollar is spent responsibly.
COTTON: This year alone, the U.S. has withdrawn from more than 30 UN entities, arguing some were filled with anti-Israeli bias and a threat to America and its allies. Waltz points to the United Nations Relief and Workers Agency for Palestinian Refugees.
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: Secretary Rubio himself said UNRWA was completely infiltrated by Hamas. We saw a number of their employees take part in October 7.
COTTON: But critics warn U.S. efforts to reform the UN could diminish the country’s voice on the world stage.
HOUSE APPROPRIATIONS COMMITTEE RANKING MEMBER ROSA DELAURO: Our participation of the United Nations affords us immense influence over international affairs, influence which will undoubtedly be claimed by our adversaries should we choose to forfeit it.
COTTON: Waltz says, even with the Trump administration’s budget cuts, the U.S. rather, remains the largest contributor to the UN.
