We are very disappointed that a delegation would seek to prevent a simple vote from being held on a resolution, which was duly submitted according to the requirements of the Commission. This motion would create a troubling precedent and not only erodes the working methods of this Commission but of all UN bodies. The contents of the U.S. resolution reflect proposals that the United States has been making since mid-February and also made in the first amendment the United States proposed to the “agreed conclusions” of the Commission. If a vote had been held on each amendment, as requested, this resolution would not be necessary. No further consultation was needed. This was a response to the lack of votes and lack of consideration that these amendments have been shown for the past month.
