Yaryna Ferencevych
Deputy Representative of the Economic and Social Council of the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madame Chair.
Reiterating our earlier statements, filling the agenda with symbolic observances and juvenile gestures—like an International Wellness Day—divert attention from the UN’s essential work and does nothing to spur economic growth. This scope creep fuels bureaucratic inertia and undermines the UN80 “back to basics” reform effort. Let us focus our efforts instead on practical, results-driven actions.
Second, this resolution reaffirms the 2030 Agenda and Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). As the United States has made abundantly clear, the 2030 Agenda and SDGs impinge upon state sovereignty as a soft form of global governance, and we will not reaffirm them.
Third, the text references resolution 79/287 on global health and foreign policy, which the United States voted against and to which we remain opposed. We reiterate that health-related policy must remain the sovereign responsibility of national governments, shaped by domestic priorities, national contexts, and the will of citizens—not imposed by international bureaucracies or multilateral mandates.
For these reasons, the United States will vote no and urges other Member States to consider the value-add of this resolution in this time of reform.
Thank you.
