Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
ROB SCHMITT: Ambassador Waltz joins us now. Sir, good to see you again. Many of the top conservative voices, and I know you see this, are angry over this attack, and of course, it’s fun to be contrarian, and it gets a lot of attention for them on social media and such. I view it, though, as it’s sort of undeniable, that this was probably an optimal moment to end a regime that has very dark intent before it’s too late. This is an ideology, as we know, that inspires suicide bombings. These are not people that can be heavily armed, and they continue to push to do that.
AMBASSADOR MIKE WALTZ: Yeah, you know, it’s interesting, and a lot of the hand wringing out there, because President Trump has been absolutely consistent in all three of his campaigns. He was crystal clear this genocidal ideological regime run by Islamic religious clerics cannot and will not ever have a nuclear weapon. He was consistent in his first campaign, his second campaign, his third campaign. A key part of his first term was withdrawing from Obama’s horrible Iran deal, the second thing he signed in coming into office this time was restoring maximum pressure. And you know what? You know what the American people voted for? From my perspective, 77 million of them for President Trump, was a president that doesn’t accept bad deals on behalf of the American people and a president that actually stands for his red lines and stands by his word. President Trump has done all of those things. It’s been consistent with everything he has said since he entered into politics. And just finally, I’ll say Iran has been at war with us for 47 years. Hundreds of Marines in the Beirut bombing, hundreds of American soldiers in Iraq, attack after attack around the world. How many billions have we spent dealing with this problem over multiple administrations for decades? And what I love about President Trump is rather than admire the problem and hand wring about it and issue strongly worded letters and statements, he’s finishing the problem, he’s solving the problem, and he’ll do so in a way that’s decisive.
SCHMITT: And that’s, you know, that’s exactly where I want to go. You know if this regime is fully dismantled, if this gets done, and some kind of stable Western friendly government rises up from whatever’s left, maybe with the help of the Kurds, perhaps to secure that transition. What could the world look like if the Middle East is largely with the Americans and is pro-Western, and Iran’s regime is decimated? Imagine the Middle East in that moment. Describe what that could be like for the rest of the world.
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: Well, let me start with what you won’t have, you won’t have the world’s largest state sponsor of terrorism, attacking us, attacking Israel, attacking our Gulf allies in the Middle East, trying to assassinate President Trump. I’ll remind everyone on both sides of the aisle that we’re only 18 months away from those assassination temps. You won’t have them supporting the Houthis, Hizballah, Hamas, attacking global shipping, attacking global energy supplies. And what we will be able to do is shift some of those assets to deal with the greatest threat that this country has ever faced in the Chinese Communist Party. So, you know, for everyone who says we should be focused over there, I agree, and what President Trump’s action is going to do is solve the problem once and for all. We cannot have this Iranian regime with a nuke. We cannot have it with thousands and thousands of ballistic missiles that will overwhelm air defenses. You can’t have sleeper cells trying to assassinate him, his cabinet and others on American soil. Like, you know, enough is enough. And when it became clear they weren’t negotiating in good faith, he didn’t just accept a bad deal for the sake of it. He said, enough is enough, and is taking decisive action. So the world will be a far more peaceful place. You have a president that puts diplomacy, and you have a president that is going to do what’s necessary to achieve stability and peace for the American people.
SCHMITT: And then perhaps a world that doesn’t require so many American military bases throughout the Middle East and so much of a footprint for us.
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: As a former veteran that got sucked into those endless wars, Heck yeah. And we should be supporting him 100%.
SCHMITT: Ambassador Waltz, good to see you.
